<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  DMG / Developer ID entitlements for the embedded Briefcase Python engine
  ("Fichero Server.app"), the nested helper the sandboxed app spawns.

  EXACTLY TWO KEYS — the same hard rule as FicheroEngineAppStore.entitlements:
  per Apple's Entitlement Key Reference, com.apple.security.inherit is
  incompatible with other keys, and the system ABORTS the child at spawn if
  one is present. The 2026.08.30 DMG proved it live: this file carried four
  com.apple.security.cs.* hardened-runtime exceptions alongside the pair, and
  the helper died in _libsecinit_appsandbox (SIGTRAP, before main) on every
  app-side spawn. Those keys dated from the era when the DMG app itself was
  UNSANDBOXED (inherit was inert then); FicheroRelease gained app-sandbox on
  2026-08-27 (Daniel: one sandbox everywhere) and the first DMG built after
  that was the launch this file broke.

  Why the cs.* exceptions are not needed here after all:
  - Library validation: build-release-dmg.sh signs EVERY Mach-O in the bundle
    inside-out with our Developer ID, so the 800+ native wheels all carry the
    Team ID and validation passes unaided.
  - ffi/JIT/DYLD: the MAS sandbox spike (#3746) loaded the full engine —
    2,889 native libs — with no cs.* keys; and the re-signed 2026.08.30 DMG
    helper reached ready (health+registry 200, providers seeded) signed with
    hardened runtime and only this pair.

  The helper's executables still get the hardened-runtime FLAG (a codesign
  option, not an entitlement) — notarization requires it. Do not add keys to
  this file; if a clean-Mac launch ever dies in dyld/ffi, the fix is the MAS
  shape (no hardened runtime on inner binaries), not a third key here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.inherit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
